WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Alright, let's walk through how to transcribe your first file using AITranscriber.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.000
First, make sure your virtual environment is activated.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:15.000
On Windows, run venv-win-scripts-activate in your terminal.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:23.000
On Mac, it's source venv-mac-bin-activate.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Once you're in, type python-ai-transcriber.py and hit enter.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.000
The script will scan your current folder and list any audio or video files it finds.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.000
If you have multiple files, you can type A to process all of them at once,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.000
or enter the number next to the file you want to start with.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
On Windows, you will see a model selection menu first.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000
I recommend starting with Turbo.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.000
It gives you the best balance of speed and accuracy without requiring a huge amount of RAM.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.000
The script extracts the audio track using FFmpeg, runs it through Whisper,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.000
and saves your chosen output format alongside the original file.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:10.000
You can pick VTT for subtitle players, SRT for editors like DaVinci Resolve or Premiere,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.000
plain text for reading or feeding into an LLM, or ALL to get all three at once.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.000
The default is ALL, so if you just hit enter at the format menu, you get everything.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.000
That's it.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Pretty straightforward once the environment is set up.

